## The one rule

{{ if $py -}}
`make check` must pass before every commit. It installs the exact packages in
`package-lock.json` and `uv.lock` (and fails when `uv.lock` is stale), then
runs Prettier and `ruff format` (check), ESLint with `eslint.config.mjs`
(typescript-eslint strict) and ruff with `ruff.toml` (every rule), no findings
allowed, `tsc` with the strict `tsconfig.json` and mypy `--strict` with
`mypy.ini`, the function's pytest tests and the stacks' vitest
`Template.fromStack` assertions, `cdk synth` with CDK Nag's AWS Solutions
{{- else -}}
`make check` must pass before every commit. It installs the exact packages in
`package-lock.json`, then runs Prettier (check), ESLint with `eslint.config.mjs`
(typescript-eslint strict, no warnings allowed), `tsc` with the strict
`tsconfig.json`, the vitest tests (the function's and the stacks'
`Template.fromStack` assertions), `cdk synth` with CDK Nag's AWS Solutions
{{- end }}
checks, which fail on any finding not acknowledged with a reason, and
`cfn-lint` on the templates synth wrote. CI runs the same command, and nothing
reaches `main` without it passing and a review. Never commit red.

Nothing here deploys. No command, test or script calls AWS, bootstraps an
account or needs credentials: `cdk synth` writes CloudFormation into
`cdk.out/`, offline{{ if eq .Framework "aws-lambda-py" }}, but for the wheels it bundles into the function, which uv
takes from its cache or, when the cache lacks one, PyPI{{ end }}.

## Workflow

1. Read the issue, its acceptance criteria, and your role guide:
   `docs/agents/implementer.md` or `docs/agents/reviewer.md`.
2. Read the code you are about to change and match it.
3. Write a failing test first: one per acceptance criterion, or one that shows the bug.
4. Make the smallest change that meets every criterion. One concern per commit.
5. `make check`, then commit with a Conventional Commit message
   (`feat:`, `fix:`, `test:`, `refactor:`, `docs:`).
6. `AGENTS.md`, `docs/agents/` and `.c4factory.yaml` never change. Other protected files
   change only through a Standard change (see your role guide), never in a normal pull
   request. `.devcontainer/`, `.c4factory.yaml` and `.gitea/` belong to the platform.
{{- if $py }}
   `Makefile`, `eslint.config.mjs`, `ruff.toml`, `mypy.ini`, `.prettierrc.json`,
   `.prettierignore`, `.editorconfig`, `AGENTS.md`, `docs/style/`, `docs/agents/`,
   `docs/well-architected.md`, `infra/main.ts` and `infra/standard.ts` belong
   to the Standard. ruff and mypy read their own files first, so a
   `[tool.ruff]` or `[tool.mypy]` table in `pyproject.toml` changes nothing.
   `tsconfig.json`, `vitest.config.mts` and `cdk.json` decide what the checks
   check and which CDK behaviour the app gets: don't loosen them.
{{- else }}
   `Makefile`, `eslint.config.mjs`, `.prettierrc.json`, `.prettierignore`,
   `.editorconfig`, `AGENTS.md`, `docs/style/`, `docs/agents/`,
   `docs/well-architected.md`, `infra/main.ts` and `infra/standard.ts` belong
   to the Standard. `tsconfig.json`, `vitest.config.mts` and `cdk.json` decide
   what the checks check and which CDK behaviour the app gets: don't loosen them.
{{- end }}

## Lints

{{ if $py -}}
- Fix every finding; don't silence it. A `# noqa: <code>` with the reason
  after it, or an `eslint-disable-next-line <rule> -- <why>` in `infra/`, is
  a last resort for a false positive: one rule, one reason. A
  `# type: ignore[<code>]` names its error and says why; never `@ts-ignore`;
  never a file-wide disable.
{{- else -}}
- Fix every finding; don't silence it. An `eslint-disable-next-line <rule> -- <why>`
  is a last resort for a false positive: one rule, one reason. Never
  `@ts-ignore`; never a file-wide disable.
{{- end }}
- Fix every CDK Nag finding in the stack. An acknowledgement
  (`Validations.of(construct).acknowledge({ id, reason })`) is a last resort:
  on the construct the finding's path names, for the one finding (`AwsSolutions-IAM5[Resource::...]`,
  never a whole rule on a stack), with a reason a reviewer can check.
- `make fmt` formats; never hand-format.
{{- if $py }}
- The function's packages are Python: add them with `uv add '<package>==<version>'`
  (`uv add --dev` for tools and type stubs), and commit `pyproject.toml` with
  `uv.lock`. The stack bundles what `uv.lock` pins, without the dev group,
  into the function, boto3 included, not the runtime's copy. Prefer the
  standard library and boto3; then few, maintained, well-known packages with
  a licence that fits, with wheels for arm64 Linux: the bundle installs
  wheels only. The CDK app's packages are npm `devDependencies`: add them with
  `npm install --save-exact --save-dev` and commit `package-lock.json`.
{{- else }}
- Add packages with `npm install --save-exact` (`--save-dev` for tools), and
  commit `package-lock.json`. esbuild bundles what the function imports into
  one file, the SDK included, so `node_modules` never ships; what the function
  imports at run time is a `dependency`, tools and the CDK are
  `devDependencies`. Prefer Node's own modules and the AWS SDK v3's modular
  clients (`@aws-sdk/client-*`, `@aws-sdk/lib-dynamodb`); then few, maintained,
  well-known packages with a licence that fits. A new install script needs an
  `allowScripts` entry in `package.json`: allow it only when the package can't
  work without it.
{{- end }}

## Review rules (lint can't check these)

{{ if $py -}}
1. **Cold start:** boto3 clients, resources and configuration are created
   once, at module scope in `app/handler.py`, and reused by every invocation;
   nothing heavy runs at import beyond that.
2. **The boundary:** every request is validated before use (path, query,
   body, headers), with untrusted input narrowed from `object` with
   `isinstance`. Errors are
{{- else -}}
1. **Cold start:** SDK clients and configuration are created once, at module
   scope in `src/handler.ts`, and reused by every invocation; nothing heavy
   runs at import beyond that. The bundle imports only the clients it uses.
2. **The boundary:** every request is validated before use (path, query,
   body, headers), with unknown input narrowed from `unknown`. Errors are
{{- end }}
   answered with the right HTTP status (400 for bad input, 404, 409, 500 for
   a bug) and a JSON message a client can act on, never a stack trace or the
   SDK's error text.
3. **Idempotency:** a retried request or event changes nothing twice. Writes
   are puts of a whole item, conditional writes, or keyed by an idempotency
   key; never a blind increment.
4. **Limits are set:** every function has its timeout and memory set in the
   stack, the timeout below API Gateway's 30 seconds and above what the work
   needs; SDK calls stay within it. The stage is throttled.
5. **Logs:** structured JSON, one entry per line, through the `Log` the
   function is given; an entry names the request id and what failed. Never
   log a secret, a token or a request body with user data.
6. **Least privilege:** permissions come from CDK grants
   (`table.grant(fn, 'dynamodb:GetItem')`, `grantReadData`), narrowed to the
   calls the code makes; never an IAM statement with `*` or `service:*`
   actions, never an AWS managed policy on a function's role.
{{- if $py }}
7. **Types:** no `Any` (narrow `object` at the boundary, checked at runtime
   when the input is untrusted); no `cast` to silence mypy outside tests'
   fakes; `TypedDict` for the event and response shapes; `match` over a
   closed set ends in `assert_never`. TypeScript in `infra/` follows
   `docs/style/typescript.md`: no `any`, no `as` to silence the compiler.
8. **Pure modules:** logic lives in modules that don't import boto3, tested
   with plain pytest; one module wraps each AWS service behind a `Protocol`
   the tests fake; `app/handler.py` only wires them.
9. **Tests:** pytest functions in `tests/test_*.py`, `@pytest.mark.parametrize`
   tables with `ids`, names that say what is expected; no real timers,
   network or shared state between tests.
{{- else }}
7. **Types:** no `any` (narrow `unknown` at the boundary, checked at runtime when
   the input is untrusted); no non-null assertion without a disable comment
   saying why; no `as` to silence the compiler; exhaustive switches over unions.
8. **Pure modules:** logic lives in modules that import neither the AWS SDK
   nor the `aws-lambda` types, tested with plain vitest; one module wraps each
   AWS service behind an interface the tests fake; `src/handler.ts` only
   wires them.
9. **Tests:** next to the code (`foo.test.ts`), `it.each` tables, names that say
   what is expected; no real timers, network or shared state between tests.
{{- end }}
   No test calls AWS: SDK clients are stubbed behind their interface, and
   stacks are checked with `Template.fromStack` assertions.
10. **Performance:** when the issue or the system's needs state a budget, a test
    or benchmark measures it on realistic input and the pull request gives the
    numbers.
11. **The stack:** data is encrypted at rest, every log group has a retention,
    every stateful resource an explicit removal policy (`RETAIN` for data), and
    every stack the Standard's tags; `infra/standard.test.ts` checks these. No
    secret in an environment variable or the template: secrets are read from
    Secrets Manager or Parameter Store at run time. Nothing in the app needs
    an AWS account to synthesize: no lookups, no `env` from the environment.
{{- if $py }}
12. **Releases:** user-visible changes get a `CHANGELOG.md` entry under
    "Unreleased"; the version in `pyproject.toml` changes only in a release issue.
13. **Names:** Python: `snake_case` functions and values, `PascalCase`
    classes, `UPPER_CASE` module constants, files `snake_case.py`; the event's
    own keys keep AWS's spelling (`routeKey`). TypeScript: `lowerCamelCase`
    values, `UpperCamelCase` types, files `snake_case.ts`.
{{- else }}
12. **Releases:** user-visible changes get a `CHANGELOG.md` entry under
    "Unreleased"; the version in `package.json` changes only in a release issue.
13. **Names:** `lowerCamelCase` values, `UpperCamelCase` types, `CONSTANT_CASE`
    module constants, acronyms as words (`HttpUrl`); files `snake_case.ts`.
{{- end }}
14. **No dead code:** no commented-out code, speculative options or "for later"
    features; no TODO without an issue number.

